# ── Database ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Local: `docker compose up -d postgres` then use the line below.
# Staging runs against `homestead_mortgages_staging` on the project's own
# instance, injected from Secret Manager by the deploy workflow — never set
# that here.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://homestead_mortgages:homestead_mortgages@localhost:5433/homestead_mortgages"

# The tests do NOT mock the database. They run against `<database>_test`,
# derived from the line above, so `npm test` cannot truncate the file you were
# half way through. Create it once with `npm run db:test:setup`, and again
# after any new migration.
#
# Set this to run a SECOND suite at the same time as the first. One database
# cannot hold two runs — each truncates every table between tests — so the
# suite makes the second one wait on an advisory lock. Its own database is how
# it stops waiting, and `db:test:setup` creates whatever this names:
#
#   TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://homestead_mortgages:homestead_mortgages@localhost:5433/hm_mine
# TEST_DATABASE_URL=

# ── API ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PORT=8080
NODE_ENV=development
CORS_ORIGIN="http://localhost:5173"

# Express `trust proxy` as a HOP COUNT, never `true`. The consent records this
# product writes are only evidence because the IP on them cannot be forged by
# the signer. Unset locally (no proxy); the deploy sets it for Cloud Run.
# TRUST_PROXY=1

# ── Connectors ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Only "fixture" is implemented. "sandbox" and "production" throw at boot
# rather than falling back, so a misconfigured deploy fails loudly instead of
# serving fixture data to a real borrower.
CONNECTOR_MODE=fixture

# ── Which adapter serves each connector ─────────────────────────────────────
# Each defaults to the fixture. Vendors arrive one at a time, so these are
# per-connector rather than one global switch.
#
#   PROPERTY_DATA_PROVIDER=fixture | google_places
#   IDENTITY_PROVIDER=fixture | stripe
#   BANK_PROVIDER=fixture | plaid
# PROPERTY_DATA_PROVIDER=google_places

# Google Places, for address autocomplete on screen 1. Restrict the key to the
# Places API and to this service's origins — an unrestricted Maps key sitting in
# a bundle is somebody else's bill.
# GOOGLE_PLACES_API_KEY=

# ── Stripe ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The SANDBOX keys are preferred deliberately, and the code will refuse a live
# key for identity verification unless STRIPE_ALLOW_LIVE_IDENTITY=true.
#
# This is not about the cost per verification. Live mode collects a real
# government ID and a real face scan from every person who walks the flow. Face
# geometry is biometric data, with consent, notice and retention duties under
# Illinois BIPA and its equivalents — and BIPA carries a private right of
# action. This deployment has no retention policy. Test mode proves the whole
# integration against Stripe's sample documents and collects nothing real.
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY_SANDBOX=sk_test_...
# STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY_SANDBOX=pk_test_...

# Where Stripe returns the borrower after the hosted identity flow.
# PUBLIC_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173

# ── Plaid ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The twelve-month report behind screen 3. This must be a CRA-enabled account,
# not just Assets: CRD-017 wants "a 12-month asset verification report from an
# authorized DU vendor", and it is the CRA products (cra_base_report,
# cra_income_insights) that are issued as consumer reports and carry that
# status. Plain Assets returns the same transactions and satisfies nothing.
#
# CRA is enabled per account and is not on by default. Sandbox needs no
# approval; production does. A client id that works for Assets will fail at
# /link/token/create the moment the CRA products are named.
# PLAID_CLIENT_ID=
# PLAID_SECRET=
# PLAID_ENV=sandbox
# BANK_PROVIDER=plaid
#
# PLAID_PRODUCT=cra | assets  (default cra)
#
# "assets" is the stand-in while CRA access is being granted. It is a real
# bank login returning real transactions, and it is NOT a consumer report — so
# it reports vendorAuthorizedForDu:false and CRD-017 correctly stays
# unsatisfied, income is inferred from deposits rather than determined by a
# vendor, and INC-002 is not claimed. The flow becomes walkable without the
# decision resting on evidence that does not carry the status it needs.
# PLAID_PRODUCT=assets

# Where an OAuth bank returns the borrower. LEAVE UNSET until the exact value
# is registered under Developers > API > Allowed Redirect URIs in the Plaid
# dashboard: Plaid rejects /link/token/create with INVALID_FIELD when it is
# not on that list, and it rejects it for EVERY link token, not only the OAuth
# ones. An unregistered value here takes screen 3 down completely.
#
# Unset, every non-OAuth institution works and OAuth ones are unavailable.
# Must be https outside Sandbox, and must carry no query string and no "#".
# PLAID_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:5173/plaid/return

# Encrypts vendor bearer credentials at rest, and the authenticator secrets
# behind the second step of sign-in — base64 of 32 random bytes:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
#
# There is no default and there is no fallback in production. A Plaid access
# token reads a named person's bank transactions on demand, for as long as the
# item lives, with no further action by them, and a TOTP secret mints every
# sign-in code from then on — so a missing key is a boot failure, not a reason
# to write plaintext into a table. Outside production the authenticator
# secrets fall back to a fixed development key, the way the session secret
# does, so `npm run dev` can walk the enrollment without one; the Plaid token
# store never falls back.
# VENDOR_TOKEN_KEY=

# ── Authentication ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Google sign-in is the only way in. The client id is public by design (it is
# embedded in the page), so it is a plain variable, not a secret.
#
# The real value is committed here on purpose: an OAuth client id is public by
# design, and having it means `npm run dev` gives you real Google sign-in
# rather than the developer shortcut. http://localhost:5173 is a registered
# origin. There is no client secret in this flow to leak alongside it.
#
# Comment it out and the sign-in page offers a local developer shortcut
# instead. That shortcut is unavailable when NODE_ENV=production, and the
# server refuses to boot in production without a real client id.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=193197012613-o0adqhniuai69526cp2s6ltms90l5eat.apps.googleusercontent.com

# Optional domain restriction on sign-in. UNSET MEANS ANYONE with a Google
# account may sign in, which is the current intent while the prototype is being
# tested outside the company. Set it to narrow the audience again.
# ALLOWED_DOMAIN=trywalt.ai

# Signs the session cookie. Development falls back to an obvious placeholder;
# production must supply a real one or sessions could be forged.
# SESSION_SECRET=

# Which fixture borrower to run as. See packages/connectors/src/fixtures.
#   clean_w2         — everything connects, no uploads needed
#   thin_file_renter — two tradelines, 12 months of rent, unsourced deposit
#   variable_income  — commission income, employment gap, gift funds, dispute
FIXTURE_PERSONA=clean_w2

# ── Design surfaces ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# /states renders every application and loan state with its copy, side by side,
# so the wording can be judged at its real length next to its neighbours. It is
# a design surface rather than a feature: it shows states most of which have no
# column behind them yet, it is behind sign-in as well as this flag, and the
# figures in it are invented. On for staging; off unless you ask for it.
# STATE_GALLERY=true

# The sign-in page offers a handful of sample borrowers, each a real row in a
# real state, and picking one mints a real session with no Google credential.
# A session signed in that way reads everything a tester may read and changes
# nothing at all. On for staging; MUST NEVER be set on a real production
# deploy. Turning it on locally is the only way to see the picker — the routes
# behind it are not mounted otherwise.
# DEMO_PERSONAS=true

# ── Who originates ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# URLA Section 9, on every application from the moment it exists. Public
# identifiers, printed on every disclosure. Unset, placeholders stand in and a
# production submission is refused against them; /api/health reports which.
# ORIGINATION_COMPANY_NAME=
# ORIGINATION_COMPANY_NMLS_ID=
# LOAN_ORIGINATOR_FIRST_NAME=
# LOAN_ORIGINATOR_LAST_NAME=
# LOAN_ORIGINATOR_NMLS_ID=

# ── Product ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The sheet has no product-selection screen, but DTI, pricing and the APR
# compliance tests all need a note rate. The system quotes this rather than
# asking — and WHICH product it quotes is the whole of what configuration
# decides. The rate and the term come off the pricing port's answer.
#
# DEFAULT_NOTE_RATE and DEFAULT_TERM_MONTHS were here and are gone: one
# variable priced every borrower on the deployment, and the second could
# disagree with the product's own term.
#
# The code has to name a row in `loan_products`, which is where the seven things
# a DU submission says about the loan being applied for live — whether it is
# conventional, how it amortizes, and whether it builds, balloons, pays interest
# only, amortizes negatively or carries a prepayment penalty.
# `loan_files.product_code` is a foreign key, so a code with no row behind it
# fails at file creation rather than at the casefile: there is nothing honest to
# emit for a product nobody has said anything about. Add the row before changing
# this — and a row that exists is not editable afterwards once anybody has been
# quoted on it, so a product on different terms is a new code.
QUOTED_PRODUCT_CODE=CONF-30-FIXED

# ── Servicing (apps/servicing, Doug's runtime on a database of its own) ─────
# His tables collide with ours by name, so this is never DATABASE_URL. The
# app maps these onto the DATABASE_URL, PORT and API_TOKEN his config reads;
# see apps/servicing/scripts/run.mjs. `npm run servicing:db:setup` creates the
# database and applies his migrations.
SERVICING_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://homestead_mortgages:homestead_mortgages@localhost:5433/homestead_servicing
# SERVICING_PORT=8090
# SERVICING_API_TOKEN=dev-token        # the default outside production
# SERVICING_TEST_DATABASE_URL=         # default: TEST_DATABASE_URL with the name swapped to supermortgage_test
